#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548102} :activity/id #uuid "cd35c901-d16d-5c03-8a21-e3776db75c55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916 :topic/id #uuid "cbfc5fbb-c887-5448-b39f-e7818405fa55" :topic/math-academy-id 3711} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0df5d3-d93a-5f24-a344-46796aad3425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414834} {:answer/id #uuid "7d942498-56d2-5693-bfad-80844fd7484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414866} {:answer/id #uuid "ab090754-b66d-50d4-b567-d44fb10bf4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414883} {:answer/id #uuid "b194be39-9f92-58e1-8d86-d43308338769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414885} {:answer/id #uuid "c53f8b68-f2cc-5a88-83ab-422d342a00aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414834} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f24906-9587-5d21-abdc-d9f2ac613180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78546b3f-b852-5bf0-8988-10eb32837ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-125028" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ traversed from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(4,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9})j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j)\\cdot (i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 1+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2849169c-1b7a-5c05-a4bd-188ece0a2563" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548185}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fd546e-6850-53e8-ae1a-3f4f4b165e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342387} {:answer/id #uuid "2b35bb1a-43d0-59e2-a75f-6da5905e2d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342598} {:answer/id #uuid "2d466b44-fd60-581c-aea2-39afd97d6ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342615} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d7dc1f-2c50-5c7c-88af-db176d4c240b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343851} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3bdff6-e0d5-5365-ae66-b27cbfab7afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344486} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cefcc8e-0177-5dec-979d-65559f35b4e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1c70d6fc-ab49-5aab-b1d0-4a65a7ea2b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77032" :question/problem "![](images/08/08c3195e7325d853f61562b1f62460cb0796f14377286228f2b7cd40983202ac.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=3\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$  traversed from the point $A(4,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =3\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(3\\,i+\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,dt \\\\  & =[12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(12+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f7211ec-a536-516d-9314-333e11173e9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548123}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09128715-0b6a-5c9a-b7c4-82cfe1932e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414832} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6a55ec-5d2e-5ff0-b433-60144af0675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414854} {:answer/id #uuid "64f18f1c-5e0b-5164-a6fc-70267c5edda2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414857} {:answer/id #uuid "76c57faf-8ecf-5560-9acf-a26154142b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414862} {:answer/id #uuid "c895dd03-f0b4-5188-9445-aa181d036f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414891} :answer-field/id #uuid "84019ffa-a424-5feb-9be8-03a303ad719a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2a8909b1-1a48-5c56-ac06-8b954196fc3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124936" :question/problem "Evaluate ${∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={-t|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a60622a-a8f4-5235-866d-1783f6fea36e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2b4e96-c35f-5329-ad67-9ac58f667665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414833} {:answer/id #uuid "5d05fea1-c2dc-5ed1-983c-3cd1bdea51ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414853} {:answer/id #uuid "63baee51-af86-53ee-921b-9301ac9943be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414856} {:answer/id #uuid "d8247ec2-f7ab-5814-8d82-ad9c6d067e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414894} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b7ad7b-8dd4-56ba-9f6e-701e837c4775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414894} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d6c2d2-5cd3-5ed9-a60d-0559257216bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "019e6ad0-f5c1-533f-a132-64a664a1964d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125235" :question/problem "Evaluate ${∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=-x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b7e12196-eecb-5d94-b41c-ed8d2ca8da33"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548185 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02315312-3684-5faa-a71f-6939225f794e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4{e}^{2t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414828} {:answer/id #uuid "775c713e-b491-5879-ae04-90cb3115c486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}(1+2{e}^{2t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414863} {:answer/id #uuid "d174d4e7-af1d-58cf-bd63-b6b4febdb84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414892} {:answer/id #uuid "da2b15aa-cb0c-58bb-a375-02709c3833e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414896} {:answer/id #uuid "e445ee3a-80b3-5bb3-ba14-012400aa2c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(1+{e}^{2t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414828} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f1f87b-e755-58bb-9c62-b95f8a22f0a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e11373c8-2017-5040-89cd-e12dc0c5ae7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125115" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{2x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,{e}^{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})j \\\\  & =i+2{e}^{2t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (i+2{e}^{2t}\\,j) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot 1+2t\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}+4t{e}^{2t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4{e}^{2t})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f6dd8476-1db6-56f8-b873-8fa80a97d47e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548151}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions Over General Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547309}]}
